I think that's a reasonable approach in the short-term. In the
long-term, I think a package along the same lines as plyr but for
modifying database tables could be really interesting and useful.

Hadley

On Mon, Jan 20, 2014 at 12:48 PM, Carson Sievert <cpsie...@gmail.com> wrote:
> Nevermind. This is actually quite a simple operation and I shouldn't be
> wasting everyone's time:
>
> library(pitchRx)
> dat <- scrape(start = "2013-08-01", end = "2013-08-01")
> library(dplyr)
> my_db <- src_sqlite("my_db.sqlite3")
> dbWriteTable(my_db$con, value = dat$pitch, name = "testing", append = TRUE)
> tbl(my_db, "testing")
> dbWriteTable(my_db$con, value = dat$pitch, name = "testing", append = TRUE)
> tbl(my_db, "testing") #twice as large
>
> -Carson
>
>
> On Mon, Jan 20, 2014 at 12:04 PM, Carson Sievert <cpsie...@gmail.com>
> wrote:
>>
>> Hi Hadley,
>>
>> I have an R package which allows me to collect baseball data based on a
>> window of dates.
>>
>> library(devtools)
>> install_github('XML2R','cpsievert')
>> install_github('pitchRx','cpsievert')
>> library(pitchRx)
>> dat <- scrape(start = "2013-08-01", end = "2013-08-01")
>>
>> New data becomes available as more games are played. In the past, I
>> appended new data to existing tables in a MySQL database like so:
>>
>> library(RMySQL)
>> drv <- dbDriver("MySQL")
>> MLB <- dbConnect(drv, user="your_user_name", password="your_password",
>> dbname="your_database_name", host="your_host") dbWriteTable(MLB, value =
>> dat$pitch, name = "pitches", append = TRUE)
>>
>> It would be really nice to have something similar in dplyr for SQLite.
>>
>> Thanks,
>>
>> -Carson
>>
>>
>> On Mon, Jan 20, 2014 at 8:09 AM, Hadley Wickham <h.wi...@gmail.com>
>> wrote:
>>>
>>> Hi Carson,
>>>
>>> So far, I've steered away from making database modifying functions a
>>> core part of dplyr - copy_to() is mainly there to create test data and
>>> for doing cross-source joins (in which case it creates a temporary
>>> table). Would you mind outlining in a bit more detail why you want
>>> append_to() ?
>>>
>>> Hadley
>>>
>>> On Sat, Jan 18, 2014 at 3:17 PM, Carson Sievert <cpsie...@gmail.com>
>>> wrote:
>>> > Hi there,
>>> >
>>> > I'm wondering if there is a simple way to append to a remote database
>>> > using
>>> > dplyr's framework. For example, suppose I already have the `hflights`
>>> > table
>>> > in `my_db`.
>>> >
>>> > library(hflights)
>>> > my_db <- src_sqlite("my_db.sqlite3", create = T)
>>> > hflights_sqlite <- copy_to(my_db, hflights, temporary = FALSE, indexes
>>> > =
>>> > list( c("Year", "Month", "DayofMonth"), "UniqueCarrier", "TailNum"))
>>> >
>>> > Say I want to append the `hflights` data frame to the existing
>>> > `hflights`
>>> > table. Is there a simple way to achieve this? If not, may I suggest a
>>> > new
>>> > function?
>>> >
>>> > hflights2_sqlite <- append_to(my_db, df=hflights, name="hflights")
>>> >
>>> > By the way, the RMySQL package has an `append` option in the
